The Stirling number of the second kind, \( S_{N}^{n} \), is defined as the number of ways to partition a set of \( N \) elements, say, \(\{1,2, \ldots, N\}\), into \( n \) non-empty subsets.

Show that for \( 1 \leq n \leq N \) the following properties hold:

(a) \( S_{N}^{1}=S_{N}^{N}=1, \quad S_{N}^{2}=2^{N-1}-1, \quad S_{N}^{N-1}=C_{N}^{2} \);

(b) \( S_{N+1}^{n}=S_{N}^{n-1}+n S_{N}^{n} \);

(c) \( S_{N+1}^{n}=\sum_{k=0}^{N} C_{N}^{k} S_{k}^{n-1} \quad\left(S_{k}^{p}=0\right.\ \text{if} \ \left.p>k\right) \);

(d) \( S_{N}^{n}=\frac{1}{n!} \sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{n-k} C_{n}^{k} k^{N} \);

(e) \( S_{N}^{n}=\frac{1}{n!} \sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k} C_{n}^{k}(n-k)^{N} \);

(f) \( S_{N}^{n} \leqslant n^{N-n} C_{N-1}^{n-1} \).

